<section class="section">
  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Upload a file and it goes straight into a bucket you own. A small pipeline
    runs over it, the text it found is stored beside it, and the document's page
    shows what came out. It is for someone who wants scans and PDFs kept and
    readable on their own server, without the search, tags, correspondents and
    mail ingestion that Paperless-ngx is for.
  </p>

  <ul class="card-grid">
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Upload straight to your bucket</h3>
        <p>Drag in a folder of PDFs or scans, each with its own progress bar. The browser uploads directly to S3-compatible storage; the server only signs the URL.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>OCR, optionally</h3>
        <p><code>ocrmypdf</code> with Paperless's own flags turns a scan into a searchable PDF/A plus its plain text. A born-digital PDF keeps the text layer it already has.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
    <li>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A pipeline you can watch</h3>
        <p>Every step is a row with a status, so a document says where it got to, and a failed one says why and offers a retry.</p>
      </article>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="section" id="start">
  <h2>Get started</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Four containers from a published image: Engrafo, Postgres, MinIO and a
      one-shot job that creates the buckets. Nothing to clone or build.
    </p>
    <pre class="code"><code>mkdir engrafo &amp;&amp; cd engrafo
curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cubicecho/engrafo/main/docker-compose.quickstart.yml

# The three secrets it refuses to start without, plus the address you reach it at.
printf 'ENGRAFO_HOST=%s\nJWT_SECRET=%s\nPOSTGRES_PASSWORD=%s\nMINIO_PASSWORD=%s\n' \
  "$(hostname -f)" "$(openssl rand -hex 32)" "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" "$(openssl rand -hex 24)" &gt; .env

docker compose -f docker-compose.quickstart.yml up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Engrafo is then on <code>http://ENGRAFO_HOST:3004</code>. Set
      <code>ENGRAFO_HOST</code> to the hostname or LAN address you browse to,
      not <code>localhost</code>: uploads go to a URL signed for that host.
      Sign in with any email address; the magic link is written to the
      container's log.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="readme-link">
    Full documentation lives in the project README:
    <a href="{{ site.repo }}#readme" rel="noopener">{{ site.repo }}</a>
  </p>
